  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "Strong envelope-level answer to sparse advertisement: a signed outer header exposes summary refs, class, signer, and freshness to peers that lack the inner schema, while nested signed payloads preserve evolvable content semantics. It stops short of a full chunk-feed replication design and still carries risk from lossy header projection, fixed class vocabulary, and optional schema recovery.",
    "strengths": [
      "Signed outer header makes advertised roots/frontiers/pointer summaries visible under sparse knowledge.",
      "Separate outer and inner signatures clarify carriage endorsement versus payload authorship.",
      "Unknown-pCID rules, canonical encoding, and size limits improve generic audit and bounded handling.",
      "schema_ref and content_pcid preserve an evolution path for later schema recovery."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "It is not itself a chunk-feed replication protocol and leaves feed-specific advertisement semantics unspecified.",
      "summary_refs are only a compact projection, so important replication meaning can remain opaque.",
      "schema_ref is optional, weakening very long-term audit for old unknown payload schemas."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Generic peers may overtrust header summaries they cannot fully cross-check against inner semantics.",
      "The fixed msg_class set may ossify or be overloaded as workloads evolve.",
      "Dual-signature verification and signer_ref resolution add interoperability complexity."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "Should schema_ref be mandatory for durable audit of unknown content_pcid values?",
      "Are summary_refs enough for frontier/leaf/pointer advertisement, or is another universal field needed?",
      "How should peers treat later-discovered divergence between header projection and inner payload meaning?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  },
